  'This course develops creative writing as craft, practice, inquiry, and community. Students write across fiction, poetry, creative nonfiction, dramatic writing, screenwriting, genre, and hybrid forms while learning workshop method, revision, style, ethics, publication, performance, and portfolio development.',
  $crwrjson${"slug": "creative-writing", "title": "Creative Writing", "description": "This course develops creative writing as craft, practice, inquiry, and community. Students write across fiction, poetry, creative nonfiction, dramatic writing, screenwriting, genre, and hybrid forms while learning workshop method, revision, style, ethics, publication, performance, and portfolio development.", "topics": [{"id": "8ed76313-95b5-46e4-87f2-fdbbc56ad686", "type": "topic", "title": "Writerly Practice, Attention, and Creative Discipline", "description": "The course begins by treating writing as a repeatable artistic practice: students learn how to observe, draft, keep notebooks, build habits, tolerate uncertainty, and turn attention into material.", "children": [{"id": "1ebeda14-e63f-4099-94fb-7254e7717481", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Writing as a Sustained Practice", "children": [{"id": "49e3a5c3-1d0e-476e-a36b-97b33cc71351", "type": "concept", "title": "Daily and weekly writing routines"}, {"id": "9c3536fe-5387-4095-b7ae-c1eed3dd77a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Generative prompts and constraints"}, {"id": "d1bfbaba-d439-4283-b91f-9d28c93c7f5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Process logs, drafts, and reflection"}], "description": "Students learn that creative work depends on rhythms, rituals, deadlines, and revision habits as much as inspiration."}, {"id": "45957ea3-0c48-4d39-8e39-06fbb9be3e87", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Attention and Observation", "children": [{"id": "0259059c-bf97-4edc-9683-0e98259676ef", "type": "concept", "title": "Sensory noticing and concrete detail"}, {"id": "38d0ecd8-5e4a-40aa-ad25-97ef4b9443db", "type": "concept", "title": "Place, overheard speech, gesture, and atmosphere"}, {"id": "9d7ba083-b194-4f62-9d82-1ab9d2533f1d", "type": "concept", "title": "Memory, imagination, and researched observation"}], "description": "The point is to sharpen perception so students can gather precise sensory, social, emotional, and linguistic material."}, {"id": "4e5d670f-e237-411b-94e9-8222857e0bad", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Risk, Failure, and Experiment", "children": [{"id": "47d3d2be-1e2b-4d17-991d-d284c1492d5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Productive failure and false starts"}, {"id": "a394d25a-f8f8-44ad-95f5-c70e8c93660f", "type": "concept", "title": "Play, imitation, variation, and constraint"}, {"id": "6744dacd-b6b7-4603-92f3-814a5a2f59a7", "type": "concept", "title": "Moving from exercise to project"}], "description": "Students practice making exploratory work without needing early drafts to be polished or safe."}]}, {"id": "dfa5146f-0309-4fea-9929-9ff26b974992", "type": "topic", "title": "Reading Like a Writer", "description": "Students read published work not only for meaning, but for craft: how writers make decisions about voice, structure, pacing, image, syntax, genre, and form.", "children": [{"id": "be346603-8fc1-4516-b632-e5bf9b476d56", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Craft-Based Annotation", "children": [{"id": "5604974a-7c2a-4936-8cbb-8dc199df1c38", "type": "concept", "title": "Scene openings and endings"}, {"id": "f68c0539-366a-4052-9afc-97d25dc98f30", "type": "concept", "title": "Sentence rhythm and syntax"}, {"id": "aa5c6fab-48a9-48bf-85d6-db1cf38e44bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Image patterns, motifs, and turns"}], "description": "Students learn to mark techniques that can become usable tools in their own writing."}, {"id": "556f10ce-6784-45ea-b452-02b98e036d8a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Imitation and Transformation", "children": [{"id": "3d33f74d-b14a-46cd-ac7c-d2c37df8de17", "type": "concept", "title": "Style imitation exercises"}, {"id": "5dc633c2-cb82-40d0-bff7-17a69f465a3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Genre translation and point-of-view shifts"}, {"id": "fb58188b-b084-45f7-904e-9e6590785b52", "type": "concept", "title": "Borrowing structure without copying content"}], "description": "Imitation teaches craft by making students inhabit another writer's choices before transforming them into original work."}, {"id": "7f5eca20-5db2-4c7f-8e53-703cb15452d4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Literary Lineages", "children": [{"id": "c34fceed-3236-48d7-9c78-792fe45d2ac4", "type": "concept", "title": "Contemporary models and classic forms"}, {"id": "7fb985fa-c116-497e-87fb-1e5a80cfa089", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon expansion and diverse mentor texts"}, {"id": "57ddcc44-ca58-410b-ae34-a23b00dd1009", "type": "concept", "title": "Influence maps and writer statements"}], "description": "Students connect their work to traditions, influences, and counter-traditions instead of writing in a vacuum."}]}, {"id": "75a5f0a6-ccad-4894-95fa-6248797eff07", "type": "topic", "title": "Image, Voice, Scene, and Narrative Detail", "description": "This foundation module teaches the cross-genre elements that make writing vivid: image, voice, scene, character, description, pacing, and the felt texture of language.", "children": [{"id": "6f94f167-a048-4b53-9603-f5a1a59b5062", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Image and Sensory Texture", "children": [{"id": "aa32d29f-922e-4b12-80c0-d34c58abfae5", "type": "concept", "title": "Visual, tactile, sonic, olfactory, and kinesthetic image"}, {"id": "e3402e4e-bb77-43fe-80da-062462a8e6d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Metaphor, simile, symbol, and motif"}, {"id": "7b0a372f-c7a3-4474-96ed-ee605f485d5b", "type": "concept", "title": "Avoiding decorative or generic description"}], "description": "Students learn to write from particulars rather than abstractions, making meaning through concrete detail."}, {"id": "29ad3b73-0833-486a-9062-11d9114f7215", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Voice and Persona", "children": [{"id": "20407f89-e1dd-4029-8681-51c4c09ce491", "type": "concept", "title": "Diction, register, and syntax"}, {"id": "a2fe489f-fdc9-4db8-bf6b-b9a68cb97f0f", "type": "concept", "title": "Narrative authority and intimacy"}, {"id": "fb184e7d-116e-4794-9bce-58724d628153", "type": "concept", "title": "Persona, mask, and implied speaker"}], "description": "The class studies voice as stance, rhythm, diction, selection, and pressure on the page."}, {"id": "8342666f-1fb7-464f-825e-d433d1b78608", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scene Construction", "children": [{"id": "4e3cfa49-f36c-4f96-8e0b-8df257b39c59", "type": "concept", "title": "Entering late and leaving early"}, {"id": "cfb1c7e8-fd96-466e-a2af-6a1a6e389903", "type": "concept", "title": "Beats, tension, and escalation"}, {"id": "f8028e1d-4a0f-4208-b4d1-e4d40c98b8fc", "type": "concept", "title": "Scene versus summary"}], "description": "Students practice dramatizing experience through action, dialogue, setting, and change."}]}, {"id": "4df74a4c-6773-43ac-a213-420fe22ae620", "type": "topic", "title": "Fiction Workshop: Character, Plot, Point of View, and World", "description": "Fiction workshops teach students how invented worlds become believable through desire, conflict, causality, point of view, setting, and revision.", "children": [{"id": "f214b2ba-2843-4212-bc60-d0c16dac9cc3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Character and Desire", "children": [{"id": "ae126da9-a570-4001-974c-08581705ebc0", "type": "concept", "title": "Want, need, wound, and misbelief"}, {"id": "5d3cf841-967c-4852-b56f-81fdf7a36abd", "type": "concept", "title": "Interior life and external action"}, {"id": "4eb5a044-4a3a-4065-91d3-e6521709af07", "type": "concept", "title": "Secondary characters and relational pressure"}], "description": "Students learn to build characters whose choices, contradictions, and pressures generate narrative movement."}, {"id": "c39f8f17-cd6e-4ebe-9d68-fd560fa3fef4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Plot and Structure", "children": [{"id": "44b4a0aa-b670-46d4-bfff-320b84201044", "type": "concept", "title": "Inciting incidents, reversals, and consequences"}, {"id": "d2f1037d-c33c-4ba6-9683-c8b84c504822", "type": "concept", "title": "Scene sequences and causality"}, {"id": "a58e2a0a-6002-49d5-a22d-af4dcc60484f", "type": "concept", "title": "Open endings, compression, and escalation"}], "description": "The goal is to understand plot as change under pressure, not as a mechanical formula."}, {"id": "f725bc55-d78a-4276-ba56-d8071c6ab281", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Point of View and Worldbuilding", "children": [{"id": "d9736c4b-d590-4e4e-b3e6-82950de6f494", "type": "concept", "title": "First, second, third, and omniscient point of view"}, {"id": "34ff8c53-d4bf-48a3-8afb-960244af2e8b", "type": "concept", "title": "Narrative reliability and focalization"}, {"id": "56301f5b-ed68-420f-a33a-3ef5453babae", "type": "concept", "title": "Setting, social world, and speculative rules"}], "description": "Students study how narrative distance and world details shape what readers know, feel, and trust."}]}, {"id": "89e20d98-a812-4cd1-a1d6-b54cec366a85", "type": "topic", "title": "Poetry Workshop: Line, Sound, Image, and Form", "description": "Poetry gives students a concentrated laboratory for language: every line break, sound, image, silence, and formal choice changes meaning.", "children": [{"id": "65ac9242-8ed5-4662-a808-754c02953cbd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Line and Syntax", "children": [{"id": "47a72d87-bfa6-475c-9aa4-44e95f52ffcf", "type": "concept", "title": "End-stopped and enjambed lines"}, {"id": "e385b468-b67d-40dc-bfe8-772c1ebb3761", "type": "concept", "title": "Stanza architecture and white space"}, {"id": "e84a7260-eca8-424e-b480-1f670f8dfc22", "type": "concept", "title": "Syntax across line breaks"}], "description": "Students learn how lineation creates emphasis, pacing, suspense, and surprise."}, {"id": "2978f715-cd7f-40c8-afcc-be9a7389c461", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sound and Music", "children": [{"id": "73c6956f-17e0-4513-b081-4fcd48a2ee81", "type": "concept", "title": "Meter, cadence, and free verse rhythm"}, {"id": "a6db0df8-0081-41cf-a524-43fd8a753698", "type": "concept", "title": "Rhyme, slant rhyme, alliteration, and assonance"}, {"id": "10ca5d12-5eb4-4a6e-94ae-c91793392a8c", "type": "concept", "title": "Refrain, echo, and sonic pattern"}], "description": "This subtopic trains the ear: poems think through rhythm, repetition, consonance, vowel music, and silence."}, {"id": "25f6771e-9108-4148-8655-7c60995fbba9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Form and Experiment", "children": [{"id": "951d8c2f-1581-4888-828c-1a81b142bd96", "type": "concept", "title": "Sonnet, villanelle, ghazal, ode, elegy, and prose poem"}, {"id": "c59fac13-02a7-45eb-9259-da985c5ed132", "type": "concept", "title": "Documentary, visual, and hybrid poetics"}, {"id": "aff0d972-1b95-46b6-9ed8-5ea678898d87", "type": "concept", "title": "Revision through compression and expansion"}], "description": "Students use inherited and invented forms as engines for discovery rather than as decorative containers."}]}, {"id": "f7e0a7c6-19cb-43ef-b2b5-e847b74e0a4b", "type": "topic", "title": "Creative Nonfiction, Memoir, and the Personal Essay", "description": "Creative nonfiction asks students to shape real material ethically and artfully through scene, reflection, research, persona, memory, and structure.", "children": [{"id": "64b1a3f5-eb6a-4f64-b59a-7c2fcde63d77", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Truth, Memory, and Persona", "children": [{"id": "210c2a0d-d023-494a-8f05-1abf0f7c2422", "type": "concept", "title": "Memory as evidence and construction"}, {"id": "3e859a67-7dc0-4e7b-9d58-a036f5713251", "type": "concept", "title": "Narrator versus author"}, {"id": "8079d5d2-3ec8-4412-86ad-95f4267fb661", "type": "concept", "title": "Fact, interpretation, and emotional truth"}], "description": "Students examine the responsibilities and possibilities of writing from lived experience."}, {"id": "7d5c6d0c-704e-4221-8c21-2e3e499203c2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Essay Structures", "children": [{"id": "4a31136d-6da9-4ebc-9d08-17e8ae50dba4", "type": "concept", "title": "Braided, lyric, segmented, and hermit-crab essays"}, {"id": "6c247457-d872-45b7-9ec9-3aa3e88ae4b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Scene-reflection balance"}, {"id": "ea50becf-9d13-46e0-bd24-d1689bcb9346", "type": "concept", "title": "Open questions and intellectual movement"}], "description": "The essay becomes a thinking form: students learn to organize inquiry, scene, reflection, and digression."}, {"id": "69d9c08d-6538-4c98-bdb5-8daac95d19d6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research in Creative Nonfiction", "children": [{"id": "daea3980-3604-488e-9c76-39be8359f286", "type": "concept", "title": "Interview, archive, and field notes"}, {"id": "caf21465-468b-46e1-a161-2e3546b0a78f", "type": "concept", "title": "Citation, attribution, and fair use habits"}, {"id": "b4190152-e387-470c-bb05-3e175a985e70", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethics of writing about family, community, and harm"}], "description": "Research can deepen nonfiction without flattening it into a report."}]}, {"id": "5c1f5cb1-4867-41f7-aa78-3acab397a6ea", "type": "topic", "title": "Dialogue, Playwriting, and Dramatic Action", "description": "Writing for the stage teaches conflict, embodiment, compression, subtext, voice, and the power of what is unsaid.", "children": [{"id": "c6d7094d-126a-45c2-b96d-633cc02089b4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Dramatic Conflict", "children": [{"id": "b935083e-9271-4fe7-99eb-dbab40da3d40", "type": "concept", "title": "Objective, tactic, obstacle, and stakes"}, {"id": "d0da7e66-43ee-4e7e-9842-539c1181fbe1", "type": "concept", "title": "Conflict without melodrama"}, {"id": "f4793465-c90a-4753-817a-a3e7cdf64508", "type": "concept", "title": "Turning points and reversals"}], "description": "Students learn that plays move through action, need, obstacle, pressure, and changing relationships."}, {"id": "f218f507-ca04-4da7-87e7-cae2e370549b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Dialogue and Subtext", "children": [{"id": "95f09807-33fb-4126-9fe6-c3039e4ea4a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Subtext and silence"}, {"id": "9def422d-2879-4dd7-90a9-097546becf92", "type": "concept", "title": "Status shifts and interruption"}, {"id": "93610288-937e-40c5-9c2f-d0c67492a7a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Distinct voices and compression"}], "description": "Dialogue is studied as action, evasion, rhythm, status, and desire rather than information exchange."}, {"id": "be5840c5-5d32-405e-96a1-b464084ea869", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Theatrical Form", "children": [{"id": "8a5c2259-2af4-45a7-9cd7-6bed2fb9b80c", "type": "concept", "title": "Stage directions and playable action"}, {"id": "94fbc816-7209-49e1-867a-6e3ec99fd1ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Ten-minute play and one-act structures"}, {"id": "95f4d0cb-4484-497f-937a-ff59fd9de344", "type": "concept", "title": "Staged reading, feedback, and rewrite"}], "description": "Students think about scripts as blueprints for performance."}]}, {"id": "c27dd226-a151-451a-b2fb-10922b68f915", "type": "topic", "title": "Screenwriting, Visual Story, and Adaptation", "description": "Screenwriting trains students to tell stories through images, scenes, structure, pacing, and industry-readable formats.", "children": [{"id": "e6eaf9ee-30da-45f3-9e60-a4d21f74cc73", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Screenplay Format and Visual Writing", "children": [{"id": "240f9b89-76be-4e87-bbef-a47beb9edc91", "type": "concept", "title": "Sluglines, action lines, dialogue, and parentheticals"}, {"id": "61f37556-1662-4fb1-9c87-cf59575656d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Writing what can be seen and heard"}, {"id": "54d78556-4c09-4cc9-934f-1d266c895e51", "type": "concept", "title": "Compression, readability, and page economy"}], "description": "Students learn how screenwriting conventions serve clarity, timing, and production imagination."}, {"id": "f55fc102-5e4d-4ea9-b900-1a868fe8ecdf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Structure and Development Tools", "children": [{"id": "55e3fcf7-e48d-42e6-95b0-8f34617f6fd5", "type": "concept", "title": "Logline, premise, and synopsis"}, {"id": "6b1427e1-a0ef-44db-981c-92ef6fa6658e", "type": "concept", "title": "Three-act, sequence, and alternative structures"}, {"id": "7a5cd8f9-ac87-469d-91e5-f256be76d17d", "type": "concept", "title": "Subplot, theme, and genre conventions"}], "description": "Students use loglines, treatments, outlines, and beat sheets to test story logic before drafting."}, {"id": "c1bc126c-3370-4598-9306-6ea0d97a6fe1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Adaptation and Episodic Possibilities", "children": [{"id": "a9d01859-959f-4ba0-9130-506e65ba3e20", "type": "concept", "title": "Adapting character, scene, and point of view"}, {"id": "a29f8435-cf3b-4765-8a64-a5a6368a36e9", "type": "concept", "title": "Short film versus feature versus episodic writing"}, {"id": "4d18b861-517a-433a-ae16-3c382b98ca94", "type": "concept", "title": "Pitching, coverage, and revision notes"}], "description": "This subtopic connects prose, stage, and screen by asking what changes when a story moves across media."}]}, {"id": "f44dd60f-324c-4bad-b692-77146ede075a", "type": "topic", "title": "Genre, Speculative Writing, and Hybrid Forms", "description": "A world-class creative writing course treats genre and hybridity as serious artistic tools: students explore literary fiction, speculative modes, crime, romance, horror, lyric essay, prose poetry, and digital forms.", "children": [{"id": "12c5823d-34c7-462e-9e84-dc8a4d730a74", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Genre as Promise and Pressure", "children": [{"id": "2aeb9332-d0ae-40a0-b7d5-c6db7436fe59", "type": "concept", "title": "Conventions, tropes, and reader contracts"}, {"id": "158641c2-f17c-45e3-9367-4d08d0d36244", "type": "concept", "title": "Literary versus commercial category debates"}, {"id": "54c40ea9-8154-4fa2-9f91-993c468109ee", "type": "concept", "title": "Worldbuilding, suspense, mystery, and revelation"}], "description": "Students learn how genre creates expectations that writers can fulfill, bend, or break."}, {"id": "195ee624-0d9d-4860-8236-9a1bf230a925", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Hybrid Forms", "children": [{"id": "db3b5413-483a-4246-9ef2-28d84d9ab2c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Lyric essay and prose poetry"}, {"id": "ec6bd086-e8ad-4cbf-a374-d037d4f973d4", "type": "concept", "title": "Documentary collage and found text"}, {"id": "cd8c077d-1458-4129-81c1-9d839a3e4c84", "type": "concept", "title": "Multimodal and digital experiments"}], "description": "Hybrid work helps students cross boundaries between poetry, prose, essay, script, image, and document."}, {"id": "f0c6364b-b0c3-43f8-be98-4824e98b7f17", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Experiment with Purpose", "children": [{"id": "8eadea2b-9128-4321-9fdc-ff57089de88a", "type": "concept", "title": "Constraint and procedural writing"}, {"id": "029c2de4-f496-4444-bda1-7d10bbef9342", "type": "concept", "title": "Fragment, list, archive, and erasure"}, {"id": "389fa698-a345-4b73-96c8-c72cd6816255", "type": "concept", "title": "Reader orientation and interpretive payoff"}], "description": "The course distinguishes productive formal risk from confusion for its own sake."}]}, {"id": "a7d9e760-38b1-4401-ba8e-27720afd919e", "type": "topic", "title": "Workshop Method, Critique Culture, and Revision", "description": "Workshop is the heart of the course, but it must be taught as an ethical, specific, inclusive, craft-centered practice rather than a casual opinion exchange.", "children": [{"id": "1e8ef6f5-7f5a-4d56-9892-e28939270159", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Giving Useful Feedback", "children": [{"id": "ca225e55-96a2-4ced-88a6-b5728662ecd0", "type": "concept", "title": "Descriptive versus prescriptive comments"}, {"id": "61857861-8a5a-4ab8-b7b9-d9483a5215de", "type": "concept", "title": "Craft vocabulary and evidence from the draft"}, {"id": "21662378-c0a2-4277-a06d-d220fd70709e", "type": "concept", "title": "Questions that open revision possibilities"}], "description": "Students practice feedback that is specific, text-based, generous, and oriented toward the writer's project."}, {"id": "201fa0ea-6307-46d1-b1e6-afc43d976a56", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Receiving Feedback", "children": [{"id": "ada29821-0bff-4bd8-a932-787106b84d09", "type": "concept", "title": "Listening notes and response discipline"}, {"id": "87693683-c6b3-4659-891c-a14739bce097", "type": "concept", "title": "Separating intention from effect"}, {"id": "a7cb59cb-9d7d-4f5a-8844-945be8a6f47c", "type": "concept", "title": "Choosing revision priorities"}], "description": "Students learn to hear patterns in response without losing ownership of the work."}, {"id": "67a7a140-531a-4639-a2f5-e973135ed25f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Revision as Re-Seeing", "children": [{"id": "c3f99449-4526-4179-9637-b49bf71ca9fb", "type": "concept", "title": "Global, scene-level, paragraph, line, and copy edits"}, {"id": "0334eecf-9878-4fb8-84a4-4f368500880b", "type": "concept", "title": "Cutting, expanding, reordering, and changing point of view"}, {"id": "b879f830-7c46-4d0d-af2f-a87ed13272ed", "type": "concept", "title": "Revision memos and before/after reflection"}], "description": "Revision is framed as structural reimagining, not just correction."}]}, {"id": "10a4d2a8-0203-479b-b57f-21c2bcbc5116", "type": "topic", "title": "Style, Rhetoric, Editing, and Sentence Craft", "description": "This module gives students control at the sentence and paragraph level: clarity, rhythm, emphasis, diction, grammar, rhetorical situation, and final polish.", "children": [{"id": "0e404aa6-200a-4097-afcf-8166f849e2f3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sentence Rhythm and Clarity", "children": [{"id": "c1bb37a2-2648-4bbb-9c65-0622fb3f9bf3", "type": "concept", "title": "Coordination, subordination, and sentence variety"}, {"id": "90c3fb2c-46b8-4424-b37c-549d1cd5e41b", "type": "concept", "title": "Cohesion, emphasis, and paragraph movement"}, {"id": "3c887a69-b6fa-403b-960b-70526b7effb0", "type": "concept", "title": "Concision without flattening voice"}], "description": "Students learn how syntax shapes pace, tone, and attention."}, {"id": "ce9fd63f-ddcd-4708-a37e-a3c8bb1ce334", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Rhetorical Situation and Audience", "children": [{"id": "bcc561d3-17e6-41a8-9dfe-bb940c5992f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience awareness without pandering"}, {"id": "70d19fcf-c2a0-4154-8f40-3bedae3c70c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Genre-specific expectations"}, {"id": "5732b66c-1510-4e25-861a-296fa15c90d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Voice, register, and rhetorical stance"}], "description": "Creative writing still has audience, purpose, context, and genre conventions."}, {"id": "eb729438-e0ad-4637-9551-9e00848b7057", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Editing and Proofreading", "children": [{"id": "b3b57087-6b08-4cf4-b0b1-282307015265", "type": "concept", "title": "Style sheets and consistency"}, {"id": "45f50e0d-ed60-40c2-8a3d-7ead979e8c12", "type": "concept", "title": "Grammar, punctuation, and usage choices"}, {"id": "573545d1-4f77-4315-b6c9-8ce7632e9953", "type": "concept", "title": "Final manuscript formatting and proofreading workflow"}], "description": "Students distinguish developmental revision from line editing and final copyediting."}]}, {"id": "dd65480a-9af7-4d8d-be10-449645310394", "type": "topic", "title": "Research, Ethics, Representation, and World Knowledge", "description": "Students learn to write beyond themselves responsibly: research, listening, consent, cultural context, power, and ethics all shape credible and careful creative work.", "children": [{"id": "e9052ab0-2277-474d-9e39-0196f0565f0a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research for Imaginative Writing", "children": [{"id": "39e05ea6-bed9-4bd3-bf88-0387eb35727b", "type": "concept", "title": "Field observation and interviews"}, {"id": "6d8efcc2-2e50-451e-ae8e-005e3be0878d", "type": "concept", "title": "Archives, maps, photographs, and public records"}, {"id": "b64295eb-fa0b-4fd5-9464-2bb709b17157", "type": "concept", "title": "Specialized vocabularies and material culture"}], "description": "Research gives specificity, complexity, and accountability to invented and nonfiction worlds."}, {"id": "b8671789-a92a-47e8-ab05-45f7634d026f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethics of Representation", "children": [{"id": "4f654984-b324-413f-985b-0a9ca72394b7", "type": "concept", "title": "Writing across difference"}, {"id": "602798a5-4268-48d2-8281-a15b1b833b70", "type": "concept", "title": "Consent, privacy, and vulnerable subjects"}, {"id": "e1f72bb0-e5f3-4eb8-9de3-1c689090e47f", "type": "concept", "title": "Stereotype, power, and narrative responsibility"}], "description": "Students consider the risks of appropriating, simplifying, or exploiting lives and communities."}, {"id": "6e734ad4-cbd5-43a5-8b2a-84fbfd05c747", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Context and Credibility", "children": [{"id": "675faa66-946e-40d8-80f6-c44005fc5537", "type": "concept", "title": "Selective detail and earned authority"}, {"id": "f168e2f0-a828-4193-80e9-8edc8ee1b389", "type": "concept", "title": "Fact-checking and plausibility"}, {"id": "685e8f41-2185-4214-8f6a-eda6f380c84f", "type": "concept", "title": "Balancing research with story momentum"}], "description": "The goal is to make worlds feel lived in without turning drafts into exposition dumps."}]}, {"id": "0b123a97-57a0-4846-84a8-7627af919416", "type": "topic", "title": "Publication, Literary Citizenship, and Professional Practice", "description": "Students learn how writing circulates: literary magazines, readings, contests, agents, presses, workshops, grants, residencies, online presence, and sustainable creative lives.", "children": [{"id": "1efea06f-d23d-4f25-aef2-caaa9c6edaa8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Submission and Publication Basics", "children": [{"id": "76d5422b-095d-452a-829e-6fc398b27115", "type": "concept", "title": "Cover letters, bios, and submission trackers"}, {"id": "eb224b4e-6d89-46c7-8865-1cd5da9d3d23", "type": "concept", "title": "Literary magazines, contests, presses, and Submittable"}, {"id": "e8664cd8-539c-4e64-ae2b-47462eb3e302", "type": "concept", "title": "Simultaneous submissions, rights, and etiquette"}], "description": "Students learn the practical mechanics of sharing work professionally."}, {"id": "7339d855-cc43-4f6d-b2dc-dc541062bc4c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Literary Citizenship", "children": [{"id": "3a69a205-978b-4795-9244-3799b83ba161", "type": "concept", "title": "Reading series, journals, and local literary ecosystems"}, {"id": "1824a1af-7fe7-47b0-81cd-4e30f2602659", "type": "concept", "title": "Book reviewing and interview practice"}, {"id": "ac39150c-157b-48dd-a2f3-42c61fe9a917", "type": "concept", "title": "Community agreements and professional generosity"}], "description": "Writers join communities by reading, attending, responding, reviewing, volunteering, and supporting other writers."}, {"id": "c65a3bed-93fa-4210-8d56-97c4c3d57c2a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Career Sustainability", "children": [{"id": "0a79a839-d1a1-401d-acb7-9e1c88aac0ba", "type": "concept", "title": "MFA, workshops, residencies, and independent study"}, {"id": "a21646d1-f8d0-4831-8c2c-c7789617f55e", "type": "concept", "title": "Grants, fellowships, and artist statements"}, {"id": "af6b02df-b3bf-449d-acf9-bf1fe6e877b0", "type": "concept", "title": "Portfolio, website, and long-term writing goals"}], "description": "Students plan realistic next steps without reducing art to a marketplace."}]}, {"id": "6b018775-3a50-4c83-9cf5-4deace9b7a0c", "type": "topic", "title": "Performance, Reading Aloud, and Public Presentation", "description": "Reading aloud makes writers hear rhythm, pacing, audience response, breath, emphasis, and the performative life of language.", "children": [{"id": "079aa057-64e0-4a3a-a356-bf7d14b4a79a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Voice on the Page and in the Room", "children": [{"id": "e56d9a50-6a8a-4e14-886c-58bae10d2eca", "type": "concept", "title": "Pacing, pause, emphasis, and clarity"}, {"id": "b9823676-163c-4e0e-bbff-ac9bfe1e912d", "type": "concept", "title": "Poetry, prose, and dramatic reading differences"}, {"id": "9552d83a-b668-4b5d-bc15-7f5e98d1832c", "type": "concept", "title": "Microphone, stage presence, and audience contact"}], "description": "Students discover what their sentences and lines do when embodied through breath and sound."}, {"id": "99c92057-6a80-4eff-babe-57a16db79bcb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Revision by Ear", "children": [{"id": "3ff32b61-d101-4e0f-bc31-0a2aef01150a", "type": "concept", "title": "Sound edits and rhythm adjustments"}, {"id": "e37c2fd3-6303-4a9e-8696-e4934009ad10", "type": "concept", "title": "Dialogue testing and scene energy"}, {"id": "f37faa5a-8a44-44e0-99c2-4056adbe0645", "type": "concept", "title": "Cutting or expanding for live delivery"}], "description": "Reading aloud becomes a revision tool for identifying slackness, confusion, excess, and missed energy."}, {"id": "b05c3ab2-8ef4-4d40-9752-d48942da808c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public Literary Presentation", "children": [{"id": "84d32c68-36c9-400e-ae7e-3af4b7121a81", "type": "concept", "title": "Reading selection and time limits"}, {"id": "2b895a20-0b59-4e0a-8819-be8fb7cf1d4c", "type": "concept", "title": "Introductions and contextual framing"}, {"id": "45eda7dd-9ac2-46d8-86a2-3217631969cc", "type": "concept", "title": "Q&A, feedback, and event etiquette"}], "description": "Students practice sharing work professionally and thoughtfully."}]}, {"id": "0a05d9eb-57ce-4920-a8cf-73a254136e8b", "type": "topic", "title": "Capstone Portfolio: Original Body of Work and Artist Statement", "description": "The capstone synthesizes the course: students revise a substantial portfolio, frame their artistic aims, document their process, and present work publicly or professionally.", "children": [{"id": "9c002de0-e087-4ac8-aac9-7df90c7ce006", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Portfolio Architecture", "children": [{"id": "d8575e1e-4f3e-4285-8966-f0e8df8d209f", "type": "concept", "title": "Selecting strongest pieces"}, {"id": "441c2d0e-473b-4093-9e21-1bde0925dc7c", "type": "concept", "title": "Ordering, pacing, and genre balance"}, {"id": "9edbdafc-8981-474b-af3f-9f6b3710fb91", "type": "concept", "title": "Table of contents and project rationale"}], "description": "Students learn to curate a coherent body of work rather than simply collect assignments."}, {"id": "ab5d2690-4527-4fee-8150-c13416115a78", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Artist Statement and Reflection", "children": [{"id": "b53d9043-aa07-400f-ba90-dd31c9ff0a3a", "type": "concept", "title": "Influences, questions, themes, and craft aims"}, {"id": "e95af850-8b1a-49fc-a9a4-90ffe62e6914", "type": "concept", "title": "Process account and revision evidence"}, {"id": "883b2e31-3bd2-428c-9e61-e183ba4c0757", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience, ethics, and future direction"}], "description": "The statement helps students articulate what their work is trying to do and how it has changed through revision."}, {"id": "cda5e755-cef3-4a85-859a-7bbdad7a851f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Final Presentation and Next Steps", "children": [{"id": "79a42878-e3e6-4c63-bcea-690b6a07fe00", "type": "concept", "title": "Public reading or conference-style presentation"}, {"id": "fe0ac020-e255-406a-8f3e-639ef9d04292", "type": "concept", "title": "Submission-ready manuscript packet"}, {"id": "6ce4c5c6-340c-4c6a-bba6-2505fad820d4", "type": "concept", "title": "Long-term writing schedule and next project plan"}], "description": "Students leave with a usable portfolio and a concrete plan for continued practice."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$crwrjson$::jsonb,
